WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.160
Raj: The first vertical slice covers daily reporting. Input parsing, transformation, and output work together.

2
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:15.097
Lukas: That's better than extracting every helper first. We can validate an actual consumer workflow.

3
00:00:15.097 --> 00:00:22.849
Priya: Just to confirm, baseline tests come first, right? Then Raj can be heads-down on extraction.

4
00:00:22.849 --> 00:00:29.353
Anika: I'm on it with the sanitized fixtures. Maybe the failure samples need an extra day.

5
00:00:29.353 --> 00:00:38.042
Hannah: A characterization test runs before each extraction. Afterward it compares the same input outputs.

6
00:00:38.042 --> 00:00:45.578
Raj: I'll keep the existing CLI entry point. Internal modules won't change the scheduled invocation.

7
00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:52.827
Lukas: Use small reviewable changes with passing tests. A giant final merge raises comparison risk.

8
00:00:52.827 --> 00:01:01.371
Priya: One action item is defining each review boundary. Raj implements, Lukas reviews, Hannah verifies behavior.

9
00:01:01.371 --> 00:01:10.756
Hannah: The second report remains optional this iteration. Its tests mustn't silently replace first-slice acceptance.

10
00:01:10.756 --> 00:01:18.460
Anika: Good, consumers will see a complete example. That makes feedback useful before broader extraction.

